  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Richard Ashmole Cooper entered the sheep-dip chemical business founded by his family and succeeded his father as chairman in 1913. He led William Cooper & Nephews into a merger with McDougall & Robertson, creating a wider animal-health and chemicals group. Company archives preserve his agreements and correspondence around that combination, as well as records of technical research conducted under the enlarged business.\n\nCooper also served as Conservative member for Walsall for twelve years. During the First World War he helped establish the National Party, later sitting as an Independent Conservative and campaigning against the sale of honours. He remained chairman of Cooper, McDougall & Robertson until his death in 1946, ending a career that joined inherited industrial stewardship with business consolidation and parliamentary politics.",
    "html": "<p>Richard Ashmole Cooper entered the sheep-dip chemical business founded by his family and succeeded his father as chairman in 1913. He led William Cooper &amp; Nephews into a merger with McDougall &amp; Robertson, creating a wider animal-health and chemicals group. Company archives preserve his agreements and correspondence around that combination, as well as records of technical research conducted under the enlarged business.</p>\r\n<p>Cooper also served as Conservative member for Walsall for twelve years. During the First World War he helped establish the National Party, later sitting as an Independent Conservative and campaigning against the sale of honours. He remained chairman of Cooper, McDougall &amp; Robertson until his death in 1946, ending a career that joined inherited industrial stewardship with business consolidation and parliamentary politics.</p>\r\n",
